<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augustus, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1614">1614</a>–<a href="/years/1680">1680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augustus was a son of Elector John George I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and administrator of the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. Under his father's will he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a> and other lands in 1656, founding the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>. He built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neu-augustusburg">Neu-Augustusburg</a> palace at Weissenfels and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a>, a German language academy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustus-duke-of-saxe-weissenfels.jpg" alt="Augustus, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Benjamin Block · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:August,_Herzog_von_Sachsen-Wei%C3%9Fenfels_a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Augustus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 13 August 1614, the fourth but second surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-elector-of-saxony">John George I, Elector of Saxony</a>, and his second wife, Magdalene Sibylle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. On 23 January 1628, aged 13 and already coadjutor for three years, he was named administrator of the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> by its chapter in place of Christian Wilhelm of Brandenburg.</p><p>He could not take up his post. On 20 May 1631 Imperial troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> after seven months of siege, and the Catholic Archduke Leopold Wilhelm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> assumed the title, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> of 1635. Swedish troops drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> army and restored Augustus on 19 October 1638, and after a neutrality treaty with the Swedish general Lennart Torstenson he took full control on 31 December 1642 and began rebuilding the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1643 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a> at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-prince-of-anhalt-kothen">Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen</a>. His father's will of 20 July 1652 divided the Albertine lands, and Augustus became the first duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a>. He also took over the County of Barby during the minority of Count August Ludwig; the count died on 17 October 1659, the line died out, and a dispute over his lands was settled in Augustus's favour in 1666.</p><p>On 25 July 1660 he laid the first stone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neu-augustusburg">Schloss Neu-Augustusburg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a>, built where Swedish troops had wrecked the old castle; it was unfinished at his death. On 15 July 1667 the sons of Duke Wilhelm of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> offered him the presidency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a>, and his patronage of artists and scientists left large debts to his heirs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1647 he married Anna Maria of Mecklenburg-Schwerin, who bore him twelve children, and in 1672 Countess Johanna Walpurgis of Leiningen-Westerburg. He died in Halle on 4 June 1680. Of his sons, Heinrich inherited Barby and Frederick received Dahme.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Augustus, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Peace of Westphalia in 1648 promised it to Brandenburg on the death of its administrator, <b>Augustus of Saxe-Weissenfels</b>; when he died in 1680, it became the Duchy of Magdeburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-magdeburg">Duchy of Magdeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After Elector John George I of Saxony died in 1656, his son <b>Augustus</b>, already administrator of the Archbishopric of Magdeburg, received Weissenfels, Querfurt and other lands under his father's will, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weissenfels">Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle (Saale)</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1656" aria-label="Dresden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4862 394 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Augustus, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1656">What was here in 1656? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
